{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01234 - MONDO_17255",
  "verbalization": "Dexamethasone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Panuveitis (disease) through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve the treatment of Iris Disease. The evidence suggests that Dexamethasone, through its indication for Iris Disease, is linked to Medrysone and Loteprednol Etabonate, which in turn are indicated for Panuveitis. These compounds are corticosteroids, and their use in treating Iris Disease likely exerts a suppressive effect on inflammatory processes, which may contribute to the development or management of Panuveitis. The shared indication for Iris Disease across these compounds implies a common mechanism of action, possibly involving anti-inflammatory or immunosuppressive effects. While the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated, the cumulative evidence suggests that Dexamethasone may be repurposed for Panuveitis due to its ability to modulate inflammatory responses, supported by its association with corticosteroid-based treatments for Iris Disease.",
